# DTEP-F Protocol Overview

**Version:** 0.1-draft
**Status:** Specification in progress
**Date:** 2026-08-01
**Authors:** CSDT / SolidarityLab

---

## 1. Scope

This document defines DTEP-F (Digital Transaction Evidence Protocol — Fiscal Layer), positioned as Taler Corner5. The protocol specifies the data model, participant roles, interaction flows, and evidence requirements for privacy-preserving processing of transaction data at the fifth corner of the Peppol ViDA architecture.

DTEP-F is the Fiscal Layer of the DTEP protocol family. The Social Layer (DTEP-S) addresses delivery of social entitlements and is specified separately. The two layers share the same cryptographic core — Ed25519 signing and Merkle audit trail — and are designed as complementary.

The protocol is profiled for EN 16931 invoice data in the Peppol context. Its mathematical and conceptual model is technology-neutral and applicable to any structured digital transaction type.

---

## 2. Definitions

**DTEP hypergraph** — a mathematical structure H = (M1, E), defined over temporal nodes, where transaction events are modelled as oriented hyperedges connecting the six ontological sets M0–M5.

**Ontological set** — one of the six independent transversal sets M0–M5 in which transaction data is indexed.

**Oriented hyperedge** — a finite set relation e = (h, t, c, ns, nb, s) ∈ M0 × M1 × M2 × M3 × M4 × M5, representing one transaction.

**Forgettable field** — a JSON structure for storing subject identifiers, subject to cryptographic erasure and replacement per GNU Taler DD-18.

**Coin commitment** — a Taler blind signature construction used in M0 instead of a SHA512 hash, providing cryptographic unlinkability of the invoice identifier.

**DRR record** — a signed public DTEP JSON object representing one transaction in the DRRDB.

**Analytical primitive** — one of the four functions R(c,t), δ(e), L(t), τ(H) applied over the public sets for real-time fraud detection.

**Carousel fraud** — a VAT fraud scheme in which goods or services pass through a chain of entities in a closed cycle for the purpose of repeatedly claiming VAT refunds. Deterministically detected by τ(H).

